  "articleBody": "Ever finished painting your interior doors, only to find them feeling a bit sticky or having issues with the paint adhering properly? It's a common headache, but usually preventable! One big culprit is not giving the paint enough time to cure between coats. While some paints might feel dry to the touch in a couple of hours, they often need much longer to fully harden. For instance, some specific products suggest at least two hours between coats, but the room itself might not be comfortably ready for normal use for 24-48 hours, especially if you're sensitive to odors. Another frequent mistake is painting over existing waxy or glossy surfaces without proper prep. The new paint just slides right off. Using the wrong primer, or skipping it entirely, on previously coated or repaired surfaces can also lead to adhesion problems. And forgetting to decide if the door will be painted in place or removed for easier access can impact the overall finish and dry time. Getting these details right ensures your doors look great and function perfectly. If you're tackling detailed paneled doors or those with old oil finishes, a professional touch can really make a difference and prevent these sticky situations.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}